WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:06.680
In response to the tragedy of World War
II, people from the town Reggio Emilia

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:12.059
developed a new model of education. War
ought to never happened again.

00:00:12.059 --> 00:00:17.940
Led by Loris Malaguzzi, a teacher, their
idea was to enhance a child's learning

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:23.250
through real-life experiences instead of
a fixed curriculum so that each child

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:28.320
develops a love for discovery, forms a
personality of their own and learns to

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:34.410
respect others. Land, money, food and
skills for the first preschool were

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:38.129
provided by the community. The Reggio
Emilia

00:00:38.129 --> 00:00:43.620
approach is based on the philosophy of
an image of a child. All children are

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:48.180
viewed full of potential, with an innate
sense of curiosity and endless

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:53.910
imagination. They are creative, capable of
constructing their own learning and they

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:59.010
have a natural interest to explore while
they follow their own interests they

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:03.840
also stay connected with others. Adults
nurture their learning by providing a

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:10.890
rich environment and support. Instead of
a one-size-fits-all curriculum Reggio

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:16.650
Emilia uses a child centered approach
and project-based learning projects can

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:21.750
emerge anytime, for example during play.
Teachers can observe and create

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:27.090
opportunities for new learning at a
specific instance for example by opening

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:32.189
up new environments, letting children
raise thought-provoking questions or by

00:01:32.189 --> 00:01:37.350
inviting others to collaborate after an
intervention. The children take control

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:41.790
and progress independently until the
project is finished and can be shared

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:47.189
with others. Throughout a project
children naturally embody the spirit of

00:01:47.189 --> 00:01:54.030
researchers, risk takers, designers and
explorers. They make hypotheses, try new

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:58.500
things,
investigate, play and imagine. To progress

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:03.509
the explorers raise the questions, the
researchers give feedback and the

00:02:03.509 --> 00:02:07.099
designers demonstrate ideas and
prototypes.

00:02:07.099 --> 00:02:12.750
Lastly they all turn their theories into
reality and construct experiments

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:17.280
together. The learning becomes social
with endless ways to solve specific

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:24.960
problems. To promote the development of
creativity teaches step aside and let

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:29.900
mistakes happen. The conflicts that can
follow nurture their social skills.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:35.970
Because conflicts teach children to
speak, to listen, to argue and to discuss,

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:40.920
then learn to accept different opinions
and to respect others for who they are.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:46.920
If two kids discuss a problem a third
can have an idea and the entire group

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:54.510
learns the value of teamwork. Teachers
document projects through pictures,

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:59.760
videos or written observations. The
learnings become visible in photographs,

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:06.390
drawings or sculptures, ideas, quotes and
transcripts of conversations are put

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:11.489
onto the walls. The children can later
revisit their achievements, see projects

00:03:11.489 --> 00:03:15.690
that were left unfinished and learn
that failure is part of the path to

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:21.360
progress. They built confidence in their
own abilities. But teachers aren't

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:26.370
instructors, instead they are companions
in the child's own journey of discovery.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:32.370
They can introduce books, show new tools
or offer entirely new perspectives.

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:37.380
However they always remain mindful not
to take over the constructive learning

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:41.880
process knowing that this limits a
child's endless imaginations and

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:46.560
motivation. The children are fully in
charge and develop a sense of ownership

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:54.690
of their own progress, the foundation for
a love for lifelong learning. Malaguzzi

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:59.880
believed that there are 100 languages
children can use to express themselves:

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:08.880
they paint, sculpt, drum, whisper and hark
they build, listen, speak or sing and

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:15.120
dance and they play, which Einstein
allegedly said, is the highest form of

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:19.510
research.
Children learn from adults, other

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:25.060
students, the environment which is
regarded as the third teacher. Rich in

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:30.450
materials the space is designed to spark
curiosity, creation and discovery.

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:35.590
Ideally there is an open kitchen and big
windows so the children can see what's

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:40.720
happening outside, just like at an
Italian town square, a piazza, where

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:47.560
we can observe the lives of other
members of our community. Parents are

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:52.150
regarded is the last part of this
education. To deepen their knowledge of a

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:56.560
child centered education they are
encouraged to learn from and with the

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:01.630
teachers and children so when the
children come home after school they can

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:08.620
continue to be researchers, risk takers
and explorers. Malaguzzi once said: "stand

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:13.450
aside for a while and leave room for
learners, observe carefully what children

00:05:13.450 --> 00:05:18.850
do and then if you have understood well,
perhaps teaching will be different from

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:25.000
before". Maybe one day our small
researchers and explorers will grow into

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:32.880
the big inventors and great explorers.

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:37.750
Millions of students from all around the
globe have watched our sprouts videos

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:42.010
for better learning, thousands of
teachers play them in their classrooms

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:47.440
to start projects, volunteers on YouTube
have translated them to over 25

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:52.600
languages. Our mission is to promote
learning by doing in classrooms around

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:57.940
the world if you are a great explainer
and a passionate teacher and you want to

00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:03.250
help us develop outstanding content
contact us, to support our channel with a

00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:08.850
donation visit patreon.com/sprouts.

